# tools/cl/claim_preserve_control_witness.rish -- the pass-time guard finally has a control.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `claim_preserve` has stood since 20260724 as the safety that makes a broad
# register pass honest. It extracts claim tokens before and after, counts modal terms, and
# refuses any drift. A hand invokes it with CLAIM_PRESERVE_FILES, so it sits on no roster, and
# until 20260915 it had no control. Every claim it made about itself was a claim about code
# nobody exercised.
#
# WHAT THE LAP ADDED. The modality reading diffs eighteen terms as one bag, and two populations
# live in there.
#
#   OBLIGATION -- must, shall, should, may, require, recommend, propose, seat, hold, parked.
#   This is what the tree owes, and a pass moving one has changed the tree's word.
#
#   REGISTER -- never, none, always, every, all. These are the words the register law asks a lap
#   to RECAST (`.claude/rules/radiant-style.md`, `.claude/rules/gauge-style.md`), so a lawful
#   sweep moves them by doing as it is told.
#
# The guard refused both alike and named neither.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS TREE'S OWN HISTORY. Commit f258e5f58 restated fourteen negatives in
# `skate/README.md` under that law. Run the scan with CLAIM_PRESERVE_BASE=f258e5f58^ and it
# answers obligation_drift=1 register_drift=1: `may` 5 -> 4 and `all` 10 -> 12. The `all` rise is
# the restatement idiom alone -- "A, B and C all stay outside".
#
# WHAT THE 20260916 LAP ADDED. The claim-token reading diffed BEFORE against AFTER with one
# `cmp` and printed `claim tokens drifted` with two headed lists beneath it, so the line said
# only that something moved. Two directions live in there, and one of them is the fault the
# guard is named for.
#
#   LOST -- a token in BEFORE and absent from AFTER. The pass carried a fact away with it,
#   and a reader who depended on that fact meets a page that no longer says so.
#
#   ADDED -- a token only in AFTER. Under a register pass this is still a content change
#   worth seeing, and it is never a loss.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS TREE'S OWN SWEEP. Commit 6e001803b recast `settlement/README.md` under the
# register law; run the scan with CLAIM_PRESERVE_BASE=6e001803b^ and it answers
# `claim_lost=0 claim_added=3` -- a Style line's path and two proper nouns. The refusal stands
# exactly where it stood; what moved is that the line now names which way.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal, in a throwaway pen holding a real git repository:
#   an untouched page reads obligation_drift=0 register_drift=0 and exits 0;
#   `must` weakened to `may` refuses, named obligation, with register_drift=0;
#   a negation recast to a positive refuses, named register, with obligation_drift=0;
#   an appended restatement raising `all` alone is named register;
#   both classes moving at once are counted apart;
#   a vacuous call, an absent file, a file the BASE lacks, and a moved pinned digest each refuse;
#   an unchanged page reads claim_lost=0 claim_added=0 -- which is also the regression leg for
#     an empty count, since the split's first draft counted with `grep -c ""`, whose exit 1 on
#     an empty file ended the whole scan under `set -e` with no output at all;
#   a dropped token refuses, named a LOSS, and is listed beneath the BEFORE heading;
#   an added token refuses, named an ADDITION, with claim_lost=0;
#   a swapped token counts one in each direction;
#   four mutations bite -- an unreadable negation vocabulary refuses, an emptied restatement
#   list misclassifies the restatement, flipping the derived branch misclassifies `never`, and
#   swapping the two `comm` directions calls a loss an addition -- the last because leg 10 and
#   leg 11 both refuse either way, so a refusal alone cannot tell the directions apart.
#
# The control is `tools/fixtures/c/claim_preserve_control.sh`; the reading under test is
# `tools/fixtures/c/claim_preserve_scan.sh`; the derived vocabulary comes from
# `tools/fixtures/p/prose_register_scan.sh`.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. That the refusal should ever soften. The gate stands exactly where it
# stood: any drift exits 1 -- in either class and in either direction. Whether a register-only
# drift, or an addition-only drift, may pass is a door for Keaton, open on
# `construction/ITINERARY.md` rather than taken here.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cl/claim_preserve_control_witness.rish

say "claim_preserve_control: Language EN -- a guard with no control states rather than proves."
say "claim_preserve_control: Style Gauge at Meter -- the refusal is the subject."
say "claim_preserve_control: Lens TAME -- safety first: name which class moved before deciding."

let ctl = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/c/claim_preserve_control.sh"]
say ctl.out
assert ctl.ok else "claim_preserve_control RED -- ${ctl.out_brief} ${ctl.err_brief}"

let verdict = find ctl.out "control_verdict=ok"
assert (length verdict) == 1 else "claim_preserve_control: no control_verdict=ok line -- ${ctl.out_brief}"

# The control tallies its own legs, and the tally is asserted beside the verdict: a leg written
# tomorrow must be HEARD. A verdict alone says only that the control reached its last line.
let failures = find ctl.out "failures=0"
assert (length failures) == 1 else "claim_preserve_control: failures not zero -- ${ctl.out_brief}"

let legs = find ctl.out "legs=21"
assert (length legs) == 1 else "claim_preserve_control: leg count moved from 21 -- raise it here when a leg lands: ${ctl.out_brief}"

say "GREEN: claim_preserve_control -- 21 legs, 0 failures; obligation and register classes told apart, and a lost claim told from an added one."
